\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: INTERVIEW: Mexico Carstens: Adjust Rates On Monetary Factors\b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2011\par 15:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- To set interest rates based on monetary considerations\par \par \uc2 -- Monitoring steps against inflows by others in region\par \par \uc2 -- Exports growing very strongly, to remain robust\par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments, context)\par \par \uc2 By Natasha Brereton\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 KYOTO, Japan (Dow Jones)--Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will adjust interest rates based chiefly on monetary-policy considerations, but it is closely monitoring action by others in the region to address capital inflows, its governor said Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of meetings in Kyoto, Agustin Carstens said that exports were growing very strongly, despite appreciation in the Mexican peso, and that he expected them to continue to support output growth in the future.\par \par \uc2 Interfering with market forces isn't very effective and introduces distortions, he said, adding that "so far" \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's anticurrency-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has worked well. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to "constantly" evaluate its stance, he noted.\par \par \uc2 "We are watching very closely what others [in the region] are doing and actions on capital flows," he said, when quizzed whether he was concerned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hard-line attitude could encourage additional inflows.\par \par \uc2 Asked if rate cuts would be preferable to other measures to curb peso appreciation, Carstens said: "We will adjust the policy rate based primarily on monetary policy considerations."\par \par \uc2 Policy makers have been spurred to take measures to limit capital inflows into fast-growing parts of Africa, Latin America and Asia--a trend which has been amplified by super-loose monetary policy in sluggish developed economies.\par \par \uc2 Faced with appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Brazil, Argentina and Chile have all \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Carstens shrugged off the suggestion that the strength of the peso was hurting Mexico's international competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 "Right now, exports are growing very fast and are making an important contribution to growth, because the export sector has been developing other comparative advantages," Carstens said.\par \par \uc2 "I expect it to continue to provide support to growth."\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the cost of sterilizing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was a deterrent to continuing to accumulate them, Carstens said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a very clear mandate to bring inflation under control and provide financial stability.\par \par \uc2 "We have to guide our actions accordingly," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Natasha Brereton, Dow Jones Newswires, +65-6415-4044, natasha.brereton@dowjones.com [ 30-01-11 1440GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110130e71u00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
