\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real flat; Fed pledge supports region\b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2011\par 14:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Brazil real flat after latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso firms 0.79 pct, Chile peso adds 0.8 pct (Adds comments, updates prices; changes dateline, previous SAO PAULO)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/NEW YORK, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Brazil's real shook off the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures deployed by local authorities to end unchanged on Wednesday, while Mexico's and Chile's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were supported by solid U.S. housing data.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  received further support from a pledge by the U.S. Federal Reserve that it would keep up its bond-buying program, which has boosted higher-risk assets around the world. For details, see [ID:nN25283937]\par \par \uc2 By underpinning demand for the U.S. bond market to keep yields down, the Fed is pushing investors to seek higher returns in other assets.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to have a very large overhang of liquidity," said Enrique Alvarez, head of Latin America research at IDEAglobal in New York. "That continues to point to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in Latin America."\par \par \uc2 The pledge from the Fed after its first meeting of the year was largely anticipated by markets.\par \par \uc2 Low interest rates in the United States and across the industrialized world have boosted demand for Latin American assets, driving \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multiyear highs. Regional policymakers have waded into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to protect the competitiveness of local manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> bid unchanged at 1.669 per dollar on the local spot market, although it firmed in international trade <BRL=> after the Fed's statement.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said late on Tuesday that it could for the first time ever offer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards at auction, adding to its growing toolbox of market mechanisms aimed at cooling the real's gains. [ID:nN25294325]\par \par \uc2 CLOSING LOOPHOLES\par \par \uc2 The lack of market reaction to the announcement was blamed on uncertainty about the frequency of such auctions as well as their aim, since the forwards market is tiny compared to Brazil's spot and futures markets.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said this latest measure is likely an attempt to keep speculators from trying to get around previous measures.\par \par \uc2 "The decision looks like an effort to close a loophole to capture all sides of the market," said Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, a strategist at RBS Securities in New York, in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Brazil has the highest yields in the region as it hikes interest rates to keep its strong economy from overheating.\par \par \uc2 The real is expected to keep strengthening until authorities introduce more drastic measures such as widespread quarantines or capital controls, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The next instrument (to be announced) could be some type of restriction on a specific inflow, something like the quarantine," said Jorge Lima, a consultant at Previbank DTVM in Sao Paulo. "But Brazil can't shut itself off from long-term investment, so it would have to be a very specific restriction on speculative transactions."\par \par \uc2 The Mexican and Chilean pesos both firmed on Wednesday, helped by data showing U.S. single-family home sales in December rose to their highest in eight months.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> strengthened 0.79 percent to 12.0025 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The outlook for the U.S. housing sector could be improving, and that is a good sign for the economy. (The United States) is our biggest trading partner and so this is having a good influence on the peso," said Mario Copca, an analyst at the Vanguardia brokerage in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, the peso <CLP=CL> firmed 0.8 percent to 489.90 per dollar as the price of copper, the country's main export, was supported by the U.S. economic data.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso closed at its strongest close since the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Jan. 3 aimed at keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110129e71q0008k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
