\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: BofA/Merrill Says MXN  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not Imminent\b0\par\par\uc2 1,095 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2011\par 20:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1905 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of America/Merrill Lynch sees little risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as USD/MXN hovers just above the key 12 level after closing below that level Monday. Commitment to a free-floating regime, a still competitive exchange rate reflected in narrowing external deficits, and tolerance for some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength among authorities are things BofA/Merrill sees keeping Mexico out of a "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war." USD/MXN recently quoted in Mexico City at 12.0410, according to Infosel. (anthony.harrup@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1829 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is 2.5% off its recent lows against the dollar and the analysts expect it to continue to appreciate as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on January 20 approaches, according to analysts at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . They note that the lira has been one of the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the EM space since expectations for easing by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to form in early December and were confirmed by a larger than expected 50-bp cut on Dec. 16. BBH expects the bank to continue tightening reserve requirements, but to keep rates on hold at this week's meeting.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1801 GMT [Dow Jones] Price guiance is out on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Montreal\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 5-year benchmark-sized covered bond, being sold via the private Rule 144a market. Guidance is in the area of 47 bps over mid-swaps area. The bond is expected to price later Tues. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1800 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD down more than a cent from session highs as EcoFin meetings fail to produce a meaningful headline regarding debt crisis containment, especially possible EFSF expansion. EUR/USD at 1.3364 from 1.3296 late Mon, according to EBS via CQG. Session high was at 1.3467. There is better sentiment toward the euro, but support remains tenuous, say analysts. "Despite the rhetoric, most of the decisions reflect actions that will occur much later in the year," says Steven Englander of Citi. "So there is room for slippage." He says, "we are near enough to technical levels in the 1.34-1.35 range that euro shorts are probably reflecting on whether there is a significant sentiment shift."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1750 GMT [Dow Jones]Pre-refunded tax-free municipal bonds, the proceeds of which are escrowed in U.S. Tsys, are now trading at yields above their taxable Tsy counterparts, points out Cumberland Securities' David Kotok. That leaves the same backing on both sides - the US Government - yet one bond outrades another. Why? Muni funds have been forced to sell to meet redemptions by investors "frozen like a deer in the headlights of a car" by "media hype about massive defaults." That leaves a huge imbalance between buy and sell side, which presents cherry picking opportunities across the muni landscape. "We find this period to be one of the great bond buying opportunities of a generation," Kotok says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stan.rosenberg@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1737 GMT [Dow Jones] Don't be misled by the headline number about China cutting Treasurys. They cut T-bills and actually boosted holdings of Treasury notes and bonds. It is just that the amount of selling in T-bills overwhelms the buying in coupons. Here is the detail: China cut its T-bill holdings from $45.9 billion to $24.8 billion, while increasing Treasury note and bond holdings to by almost 10 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1709 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Crown Holdings, Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said it is offering $600 million of senior notes due 2021 via its subsidiaries Crown Americas LLC and Crown Americas Capital Corp. Proceeds will go toward retiring Crown's outstanding $600 million of 7.75% senior unsecured notes due 2015. Pricing, via lead underwriter \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , is expected later Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (michael.aneiro@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1654 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telecom Italia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (TI) new EUR1 billion five-year bond, which priced at 250 bps over midswaps Tuesday, tightened 13 bps in the secondary market. The deal is the first euro-denominated bond from a high grade non-financial corporate borrower from the euro-zone periphery or "soft core" this year, says Suki Mann at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It's the sort of deal which just might encourage other [issuers] to have a look too." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1646 GMT [Dow Jones] The russian ruble hit a three-month high versus dollar, continuing recent gains. BBH strategist Marc Chandler say general dollar weakness, strength in oil prices and an overall movement to risk is helping the ruble. USD/RUB at 29.877, according to FactSet Research. (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1645 GMT [Dow Jones] Contrary to the overall flow of foreign investments into US long-term debt, China reduced its Treasury holdings by $11.2 bln. "Is this worrisome?" asks IHS Global Insight in an email note. "Not necessarily. China's trade deficit with the US moved sideways in November, providing no additional incentive to invest in US securities." Looking forward, IHS Global Insight sees ongoing support for foreign demand of U.S. securities in the form of a weak U.S. dollar, a stronger U.S. economy - boosted by the recently passed Obama tax package - and weak growth prospects for Europe and Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1640 GMT [Dow Jones] Securitization, or the process of bundling consumer loans into bonds and selling to investors, is important for "diversity of funding sources" and "critically important for consumer finance," say SIFMA analysts, speaking at a meeting. The organization is monitoring the impact of Dodd-Frank regulation on the securitization market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1630 GMT [Dow Jones] International demand for US long-term debt bounced back in November as both private and official investors poured into long-term Treasury bonds and notes, says Gregory Daco, US Senior Economist at IHS Global Insight, in an email note on the TIC data. "Foreign demand for US long-term securities has become very predictable in the last few months," Daco wrote. "When worries about the European sovereign debt crisis (re)emerge, demand for safe-heaven US Treasury securities surges." Then, as sovereign debt worries dissipate, investors shift their focus to the sustainability of the US debt, and decide to diversify out of U.S. securities into emerging markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-18-11 1405ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110118e71i000f6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
