\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Peso Ends Stronger Vs Dollar, Teases Key MXN12 Level\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2011\par 22:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's peso closed stronger against the U.S. dollar Friday, toying with a key dollar support level following positive U.S. industrial production numbers that bode well for Mexican economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted closing in Mexico City at MXN12.0390, according to Infosel, stronger than Thursday's MXN12.1425 close and its best level since early October 2008.\par \par \uc2 A local trader said the peso several times approached, then shied away from, the psychologically important level of MXN12 to the dollar, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't touched since the start of the 2008 global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Other local markets weren't particularly supportive--stocks closed modestly lower despite U.S. gains, and local government bonds were mixed. The yield on benchmark 10-year bonds slipped two basis points to 7.03%, and the yield on 20-year bonds was flat at 7.8%.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts see a break below MXN12 to the dollar as just a matter of time. With Brazil and other Latin American governments and central banks acting to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating further, a number of investors have turned their attention to the Mexican peso given what is seen as a low risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys up to $600 million a month under a system of put options, where banks can sell dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the peso is stronger than its 20-day average. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought about $4.6 billion under the options in 2010 and has bought all $600 million under the options for January. Oil dollars are the main source of Mexico's foreign reserves, which were a record $116.9 billion last week.\par \par \uc2 In a report Friday, Bank of America/Merrill Lynch said it expects the main Latin American countries, and especially Brazil, to slow their rate of reserve accumulation in 2011. Colombia may accelerate its accumulation, while reserve building should "decelerate somewhat" in Mexico and Peru.\par \par \uc2 "In Mexico, the renewal of the IMF's [flexible credit line] for $72 billion probably reduced the desire to increase reserves, but the recent appreciation of the MXN adds some upside risks to our \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  accumulation forecast," BofA/\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5980-5176, anthony.harrup@dowjones.com [ 14-01-11 2133GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110114e71e001fp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
