\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  peso seen rising as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  eschewed\b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 19:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Improving U.S. outlook also seen boosting peso\par \par \uc2 * Peso may soon test levels close to 11.80/dollar\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso, trading at a more than two-year high, could strengthen further on bets of improved U.S. demand for its exports and enhanced by the rising tide of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Forecasts for 2011 economic growth in Mexico, which sends most of its exports to the United States, are edging up, which could help the peso as Americans buy more Mexican cars, televisions and refrigerators.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's free-floating peso has an added luster as central banks from Brazil and Chile to Turkey and Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to slow gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  driven by yield-hungry investors seeking to beat returns in developed countries like the United States.\par \par \uc2 As a devout supporter of open financial markets, Mexico is the only major country in Latin America that has stayed on the sidelines of the battle between speculators and central banks.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico is the last holdout," said Clyde Wardle, a strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in New York. <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ For a factbox on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks: [ID:nSGE69503F] Graphic on region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/cat64r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/cat64r }}}\uc2  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 Investors are betting Mexico's peso will outperform other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  where authorities are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like Turkey's lira or Chile's peso, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The probability that speculators will pile onto the appreciative momentum of the peso is high, and that could quickly push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below 12 per dollar," said Benito Berber, an analyst at Nomura in New York.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Mexican peso was hurt by fears the United States could slip back into recession, but surprisingly strong U.S. and Mexican data, as well as the decision to not raise taxes in the United States, pushed analysts to revise up growth forecasts for 2011, helping the peso gain more than 4 percent from a November low.\par \par \uc2 While other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been trading at multi-year highs, Mexico's peso is only beginning to catch up from its sharp devaluation during the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 GAINS COULD ACCELERATE\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the peso hit its highest level since mid-October 2008, trading around 12.07 per dollar <MXN=>.\par \par \uc2 And gains below 12 could trigger stop losses from investors who have been betting on a stronger dollar and further accelerate the peso gains towards 11.80 per dollar, said Nomura's Berber.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  technical analysts noted that a close this week below 12.05 per dollar, the 61.8 percent Fibonacci retracement of the peso's August 2008 to March 2009 devaluation, would suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could firm to as much as 11.21 per dollar, with good support in the 11.50 to 11.70 range.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso traded between 10 and 11 per dollar in the years running up to the financial crisis, and a break past 12 per dollar may not immediately spur concerns from local authorities, said Gabriel Casillas, an economist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a meeting with analysts in New York in December, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Agustin Carstens suggested authorities would welcome the boost to sluggish domestic demand that a stronger peso could provide, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Wardle said.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eschewed the direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of other central banks, it did start conducting monthly auctions of dollar put options early last year, but these are viewed as less aggressive.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's Casillas said a quick jump to levels below 11.80 per dollar could spur policymakers to increase the its options auction. "Even then, it is very moderate," he said. (Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MEXICO/PESO (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110112e71c001bx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
