\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Peso Closes Stronger As US Fed Unveils Stimulus Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2010\par 21:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's peso closed Wednesday at its strongest level against the dollar since late April as the U.S. Federal Reserve unveiled plans to buy Treasurys in support of the U.S. economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted closing in Mexico City at MXN12.2510 to the dollar, compared with MXN12.3450 at the close Monday. Local markets were closed Tuesday for the All Souls Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 The decision by the Federal Open Market Committee to buy $600 billion in Treasurys by the end of June, and reinvest an additional $250 billion to $300 billion in proceeds from mortgage holdings, was broadly in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 The implications for the peso of Fed support for the U.S. economy are twofold, as increased global liquidity prompts inflows into emerging markets, and the peso benefits from growth in the U.S. economy, which receives around 80% of Mexico's exports.\par \par \uc2 "The peso has responded more strongly to expectations of a possible second round of quantitative easing in the U.S.," BBVA Research said in a report. "This increased sensitivity, combined with the outlook on foreign flows, favors a positive short-term trend in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 With the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing little willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the peso from strengthening, measures such as Brazil's tax on fixed-income \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  could encourage a preference for Mexican assets and invite more capital inflows in the short term, BBVA said, adding that it expects the peso could move toward MXN12 to the dollar in the remainder of the year.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economy is more closely tied to the U.S. than those of Brazil and other South American countries, and the slowdown in the U.S. recovery is expected to take some of the steam out of the export manufacturing-led rebound in Mexico. Gross domestic product grew 5.9% in the first half of the year, and is estimated to have grown 5% in the third quarter. Mexican officials are expecting domestic demand to take up some of the slack from a slowdown ahead in the pace of export growth.\par \par \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  said in a report that at a meeting with investors, Bank of Mexico Governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  indicated some gradual strengthening of the peso would be welcome as it would have a positive impact on consumer perceptions. "Gov. Carstens was very clear, stating they oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy actions which could have a damaging long-lasting impact on markets, specifically highlighting capital controls and tax measures as undesirable [although he did mention that under very extreme circumstances these could be necessary]," RBC said.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico buys up to $600 million a month under a system of put options that give holders the right to sell dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the peso is stronger than its 20-day average. At last week's options auction, banks paid the highest premium yet for the options, an indication that further peso gains are expected.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses the options to accumulate additional foreign reserves against future bouts of global market volatility. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported Wednesday that reserves were a record $110.66 billion on Oct. 29, up $19.82 from the end of 2009. The main source of the reserves is crude oil, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  changes dollars for state oil monopoly \uc2 Petroleos Mexicanos\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; 5255-5980-5176, anthony.harrup@dowjones.com [ 03-11-10 2020GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101103e6b3001gj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
