\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso sinks, Colombia unit firms\b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2010\par 17:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso hit by weak retail, bomb threat scare\par \par \uc2 * Colombia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds trigger on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso weakens 1.24 pct, Colombia unit adds 0.43\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real slips 0.4 pct, tracking U.S. markets (Recasts, changes byline and dateline, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jean Luis Arce and Nelson Bocanegra\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/BOGOTA Aug 23 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso sank to a one-month low on Monday, hurt by surprisingly weak June retail sales data while Colombia's peso firmed in relief the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not begin \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's June retail sales report undermined hopes that consumer demand will offset slowing growth in the United States, Mexico's main trading partner. [ID:nN23157622]\par \par \uc2 June retail sales fell 0.77 percent compared with May, and only grew 1.5 percent year-on-year, compared with expectations of a 4.1 percent rise.\par \par \uc2 "That's a sharp slowdown and doesn't help for consumers and it doesn't tell us a good story in terms of consumption, which is what we expect to recover in the coming months," said Italo Lombardi, a strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended already steep losses on reports of a bomb threat to the stock exchange in Mexico City, which officials later said was a false alarm. [ID:nN23212566]\par \par \uc2 Rising violence related to the government's battle against drug cartels has made the peso more sensitive to news related to security concerns.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=> sunk to its weakest since July 20 against the dollar, losing 1.2 percent to 12.9075 per dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  RELIEF\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso firmed on Monday, outperforming the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised investors late Friday by not announcing measures to curb the peso's recent rally.\par \par \uc2 Investment in Colombia's booming oil and mining sectors has caused the peso to strengthen about 13 percent this year, putting huge pressure on the country's exporters.\par \par \uc2 The peso <COP=STFX> bid 0.43 percent stronger to 1,813.40 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 However, traders and analysts were still cautious and expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could move to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar-peso exchange rate fell much below 1,800 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The trend is moderate, since traders still expect an announcement from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming days," said \uc2 Banco de Bogota\uc2  in a report.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela's announcement late last week that it would pay Colombian companies $200 million to compensate after a trade dispute also lifted the peso, said Karen Alfonso, head of economic research at the Afin brokerage in Bogota. [ID:nN20166203]\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso, which has also been under pressure due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats, bid down a slight 0.1 percent at 504.50 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Analysts doubt Chile will implement major dollar purchases to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating. However, the government could slow the pace at which it repatriates dollars from copper sales.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> shed 0.4 percent to bid at 1.765 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Sao Paulo said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was taking its lead from Wall Street due to the absence of key U.S. economic data -- firming in the morning as U.S. stocks gained but trading weaker in the afternoon when the major U.S. equity indexes turned negative.\par \par \uc2 However, the real was trading at its strongest level against the Mexican peso <BRLMXN=R> in over a month. (Additional reporting by Samantha Pearson and in Sao Paulo and Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100826e68n00152\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
