\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil, Mexico  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  gain\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 23:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Chilean peso surges 1 pct in week on rate hike\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk knocks Colombian peso down 1.1 pct\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso dips 0.31 pct on growth fears\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real sheds 0.7 pct\par \par \uc2 * Mexican bonds rally after Fed maintains low rates\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso and Brazil's real edged up on Friday after being battered through the week by concerns about slowing growth in the United States even as losses were contained by flows into the region's bonds.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso brushed off global concerns to surge about 1 percent this week as the central back hiked interest rates for the third month in a row. But Colombia's peso sank more than 1 percent since the previous Friday amid the threat of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve expressed deeper concerns about U.S. growth this week that rattled stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Some investors are betting the U.S. economy could slip back into recession after recovering from a deep slump last year.\par \par \uc2 "There is real uncertainty about the economic activity in the United States, and this will keep affecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Salvador Orozco, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt strategist at Santander in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 But investors sitting on cash were drawn to Latin American bonds. Local debt offers higher yields than the meager returns on government bonds in major developed markets, where policymakers have pledged to keep interest rates low to support growth.\par \par \uc2 More prudent fiscal management in Latin America during the past decade has put the shine on local debt amid Europe's debt troubles.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's benchmark 10-year peso bond <MX10YT=RR> shed 25 basis points this week to bid at a record low of 6.29 percent on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "We never thought we would see such low yields, but it is a function of the foreigners' liquidity," Orozco said.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> edged up 0.07 to 12.7225 per dollar on Friday after data showed rising, but still tepid, U.S. August consumer confidence and July retail sales. The peso shed 0.31 percent for the week.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> bid 0.11 percent stronger at 1.770 per dollar in the local spot market. In international trade, the real <BRL=> shed about 0.7 percent for the week.\par \par \uc2 JAWBONING GAINS\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso sank for a second straight session due to concerns local authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to cool sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains that are hurting local exporters.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso <COP=STFX> shed 0.47 percent to 1,834.90 per dollar, racking up a 1.1 percent loss for the week.\par \par \uc2 Before sharp losses in the last two sessions, the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had gained about 12 percent since early May, boosted by investment flows into the country's oil and mining industries, which also drew speculative traders.\par \par \uc2 Analysts think Colombia could reinstate daily dollar purchases that expired in June, but that would do little more than slow the pace of gains.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso closed at a five-month high on Friday, a day after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark interest rate to 2 percent, in line with expectations, as the economy bounces back from a devastating earthquake. For more, see [ID:nN12145806]\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso's <CLP=CL> edged up 0.02 percent to 509.40 per dollar, posting a 1 percent gain for the week.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has firmed about 7 percent since the end of June. Chilean President Sebastian Pinera hinted Thursday the government could cash in less dollar revenue from copper sales to offset the flows into Chilean assets. [ID:nN12124343]\par \par \uc2 But analysts looked for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to firm further on expectations of more interest rate hikes by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is seen taking its benchmark rate to 3.75 percent by year-end. [ID:nN12129420] (Additional reporting by Jean Luis Arce in Mexico City; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)(REPEAT)|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100816e68d001fx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
