\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-LatAm  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  firm on US housing data\b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2010\par 23:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Surge in U.S. home sales eases concerns of "double dip"\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso firms 0.47 pct, Brazil's real up 0.61\par \par \uc2 * Colombia peso at 9-month high, Chile's at 3-month peak (Updates prices, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, July 26 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed on Monday after data showing a surge in sales of new homes during June in the United States restored some confidence in the recovery of the world's biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's local spot market was kept on edge by concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could shift the method its uses to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to limit the real's appreciation.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso <MXN=>, which is closely linked to its northern neighbor and top trading partner, rose to its strongest in more than a week. Colombia's peso hit a nine-month high and Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  headed toward its strongest close in almost three months.\par \par \uc2 A surprising 23.6 percent jump in new home sales in June from May countered some disappointing data in recent weeks that heightened concerns the economy may slip back into recession.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. housing market is not as a bad as it was thought previously. There is a very high improbability of a double dip and that is helping \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the world," said Kathryn Rooney, an analyst covering Latin America at Bulltick Capital Markets in Miami.\par \par \uc2 Fears over a fresh slowdown in U.S. growth have hit Mexico particularly hard since the country sends about 80 percent of its exports to the United States.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed 0.47 percent to 12.67 per dollar, according to Reuters data, while Chile's peso <CLP=CL><CLP=> gained 0.25 percent. Brazil's real <BRL=> gained 0.61 percent to 1.7618 per U.S. dollar in international trade.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso <COP=STFX> strengthened 0.47 percent to 1859.35 per U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been boosted by higher expectations for oil revenue, as well as bets the country's president-elect will pass fiscal reforms that will win the country an investment grade from Wall Street ratings agencies.\par \par \uc2 "Colombia is taking a right step and we expect a ratings upgrade in the near future," wrote \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  strategists in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 The bank's analysts last Friday raised their target for the Colombian peso to 1850 per dollar for the end of the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 Gains for the Mexican peso were limited during most of the session as local banks exercised $571 million in dollar options. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began selling $600 million in dollar put options every month earlier this year to accumulate reserves.\par \par \uc2 The exercise of the options can limit the peso's appreciation in the spot market as local banks sell dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than dumping them into the spot market.\par \par \uc2 With the amount exercised on Monday, no more options contracts remain for the rest for July.\par \par \uc2 On Brazil's local spot market, the real <BRBY> was 0.4 percent weaker, closing at 1.765. Concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the real in international trade late on Friday after the local market had closed.\par \par \uc2 Traders cited chatter that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could move to sell reverse swaps instead of, or as well as, buying U.S. dollars in the spot market.\par \par \uc2 Reverse swaps are a form of derivative that would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take a long position in the U.S. dollar futures market in exchange for a short position in the interest rate markets.\par \par \uc2 Selling reverse swaps would be a way to reduce the arbitrage opportunities in the market, which have arisen due to the huge reserves the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has amassed through daily U.S. dollar auctions used to slow the real's appreciation. (Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)(REPEAT)|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100729e67q0021l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
