\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-LatAm  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  gain, stocks lose steam\b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2010\par 23:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * IMF raises LatAm growth forecast for 2010, 2011\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso up as cenbank says recent gains not excessive\par \par \uc2 * Brazil markets closed due to national holiday\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 20 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained on Wednesday, with the Chilean peso rebounding sharply and the Mexican peso supported by comments from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief.\par \par \uc2 News that the International Monetary Fund revised up its growth forecasts for the Latin America supported the view that policymakers will soon raise rates to curb accelerating inflation, drawing more dollars to the region.\par \par \uc2 Latin American stocks ended slightly lower, however, as a Wall Street rally ran out of steam and Brazilian financial markets were closed due to a national holiday.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> firmed 0.4 percent to 12.1682 per dollar after Banco de Mexico Governor Agustin Carstens was quoted by the El Economista newspaper as saying a recent surge in the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not excessive. For details see [ID:nN21183915]\par \par \uc2 "This undermines the idea that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a more aggressive way," said Daniela Blancas, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained more than 7 percent this year and that has stoked speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become more active in the market with measures such as the daily auctions to buy dollars seen in Brazil and Colombia.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, the peso <CLP=CL> rallied 1.04 percent to 521.80 per dollar after six consecutive sessions of losses took the exchange rate to a three-week low.\par \par \uc2 Traders said investors decided to take profits after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  found strong support at around 528 pesos per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Also boosting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were the IMF's new growth forecast for the region this year, which was revised up to 4 percent from 3.7 percent. [ID:nWEQ003880]\par \par \uc2 The fund said monetary policy in the region should move from highly accommodative to more neutral levels once the recovery has gathered momentum, adding that Brazil appears to be close to that turning point.\par \par \uc2 Despite the optimism in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Latin American stocks edged lower, with the MSCI stock index for the region <.MILA00000PUS> dipping 0.11 percent.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's IPC stock index <.MXX> lost 0.88 percent, while Chile's blue-chip IPSA index <.IPSA> finished near flat.\par \par \uc2 Sovereign bond prices were little changed while yield spreads between emerging market bonds and U.S. Treasuries, a key gauge of investor appetite for risk, widened 4 basis points to 241 basis points according to the \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  EMBI+ index <11EMJ>.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING/|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100424e64l000fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
