\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico peso sinks on Greece woes, stocks fall\b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2010\par 23:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Peso falls by most in 2 weeks, IPC slips from record\par \par \uc2 * Greece debt worries hit global markets\par \par \uc2 * Speculation on more cenbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also weigh (Recasts throughout, adds comments, closing stock prices)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, April 7 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso sank by the most in two weeks on Wednesday as concerns over Greece's debt problems sapped appetite for riskier assets and pushed investors to take profits on recent gains.\par \par \uc2 The peso <MXN=><MEX01> lost 0.52 percent to 12.2881 per U.S. dollar while the IPC stock index <.MXX> fell 0.5 percent from a record peak to 33,632.35.\par \par \uc2 Greece's borrowing costs hit a new high on Wednesday as the government said the country's banks sought increased support and euro-zone states argued about conditions of potential bailout loans. For details see [ID:nLDE636138]\par \par \uc2 "There is nervousness in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to Greece," said Carlos Gomez, an analyst at Invex brokerage in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Concerns of a debt default in Greece have weighed on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, as riskier assets could sell off if Europe's debt troubles spur a larger financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The peso fell by its biggest one-day percentage drop since March 24, when concerns of Portugal's downgrade hit global markets.\par \par \uc2 Despite the peso's losses, traders noted it outperformed other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and argued the peso's appreciation trend will continue.\par \par \uc2 "The market is taking a little breather," said Daniela Blancas, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Mexico City. "This does'nt imply a change in tendency," she added.\par \par \uc2 The peso has gained more than 7 percent this year, before today's losses, helped by signs of an economic rebound in Mexico and its top trading partner the United States.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts pointed to bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could soon step up its dollar purchase program, which could hurt the peso as the bank buys up dollars through option contracts. For details see [ID:nN06583990]\par \par \uc2 The peso was hurt by "speculation that Banxico could slow the recent appreciation of the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by increasing its current mechanism to accumulate reserves," wrote Gabriela Siller, and analyst at brokerage BASE in Monterrey.\par \par \uc2 In stock trading, shares in Grupo Mexico <GMEXICOB.MX> fell 2.88 percent to 34.77 pesos after gaining almost 5 percent on Tuesday while cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  lost 3.22 percent to 12.93 pesos. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MEXICO/ (UPDATE 1)_|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100407e647001dv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
