\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Stocks Chalk Up Second Straight Record-High Close\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2010\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexican stocks rose to a second consecutive record-high close Tuesday as investors continued to bet on the economic recovery and the peso reached its strongest level against in the dollar in 18 months.\par \par \uc2 The stock market's IPC index of leading shares closed up 0.5% at 33801 points. Volume was a moderate 162 million shares worth 4.94 billion pesos ($404 million).\par \par \uc2 After setting an all-time intraday high of 33,822, the IPC ceded some ground as the Dow Jones Industrials slipped back into the red and ended about flat.\par \par \uc2 Positive U.S. economic data this week, as well as last Friday's U.S. employment report for March, gave additional fuel to the local market as investors expect the U.S. economic recovery to be reflected in Mexico and in company earnings.\par \par \uc2 The Monex brokerage said in a report that the IPC could move above 34000 points.\par \par \uc2 "Most of the price patterns of the different stocks that make up the IPC show highly positive movements," Monex said.\par \par \uc2 Mining and railways company Grupo Mexico (GMEXICO.MX) B shares led the charge, rising 4.7% to MXN35.80 after \uc2 UBS\uc2  initiated coverage of the stock with a buy recommendation.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  cited "near-term expectations for $4 copper" and a potential separate listing of Grupo Mexico's railways business support expectations for share-price gains. \uc2 UBS\uc2  set its price target at MXN44.\par \par \uc2 IPC heavyweight \uc2 America Movil\uc2  (AMX) L shares rose 0.1% to MXN31.66, and cement and building materials company \uc2 Cemex\uc2  (CX) CPO shares rose 0.5% to MXN13.36, adding to the previous day's gains.\par \par \uc2 The peso appreciated for a seventh straight session against the U.S. dollar, closing in Mexico City at MXN12.2160. It was the strongest close early October, 2008, just before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  embarked on a year-long series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market selling dollars at the height of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Now the Bank of Mexico is buying dollars to bolster the country's foreign reserves against future market debacles via a series of monthly put options for $600 million under which banks can sell dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the peso is appreciating.\par \par \uc2 On Monday banks exercised $501 million of the $600 million for April.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's foreign reserves rose by $1.2 billion last week to a record $95.68 billion, mostly from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  changing dollars for state oil monopoly \uc2 Petroleos Mexicanos\uc2 . Oil remains the biggest source of Mexican foreign reserves, and rising crude prices also contribute to peso strength.\par \par \uc2 The recent market rally hasn't been without voices of caution, as liquidity from fiscal and monetary stimulus in developed countries has contributed to emerging market gains, and will eventually be withdrawn.\par \par \uc2 ING Mexico said in a report that it considers peso positions to be "saturated in the near term," and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is vulnerable to a correction in equities.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; 5255-5980 5176; anthony.harrup@dowjones.com [ 06-04-10 2023GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852 5047   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100406e6460016c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
