\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Emerging market  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  show short-term cracks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss  \par \uc2 851 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 19:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 30 (Reuters) - Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been all the rage the past year given their robust economies, but it seems these assets have become less appealing to some investors at least in the short term.\par \par \uc2 After out-sized gains in 2009, these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Brazilian real, the Chilean peso, and others in Asia, have slipped against the U.S. dollar in 2010, and strategists say these assets are showing some vulnerability as their yields have started to fall.\par \par \uc2 Most fund managers, though, are still gung-ho on EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the medium to long term, but some say it has become harder to extract gains in the short term for these normally buoyant units.\par \par \uc2 Many investors made a fortune using carry trades -- borrowing in a low-interest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the dollar and lending in a high-interest one such as the real, and pocketing the rate difference. But these trades no longer seem to be sure-fire money-makers.\par \par \uc2 "Risk-adjusted carry in emerging markets by historical standards is not attractive at the moment," said Ron Leven, executive director for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in New York. "And it's due to a combination of the compression of rates and rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility."\par \par \uc2 Leven cited the case of the Brazilian real, which has a risk-adjusted carry of 45 percent, based on \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  estimates, which means its carry rates had been better than current levels 55 percent of the time the last five years.\par \par \uc2 "Historically, 45 percent is just average -- not terrible, but not great," Leven said.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's capital controls, instituted in October last year to slow the real's appreciation, have pressured the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate in the forward market, analysts said, reducing its carry yield against, say, the U.S. dollar and euro.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRL=> has struggled against the dollar this year, falling nearly 3 percent, after rising 33 percent versus the U.S. dollar in 2009. Yet it remains the darling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  world.\par \par \uc2 EM NO LONGER CHEAP\par \par \uc2 "Certainly emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term are not as attractive as they were when they got crushed during the Lehman collapse. Brazil, for one, isn't particularly cheap at the current level," said David Gerstenhaber, president of New York-based macro hedge fund Argonaut Capital Management.\par \par \uc2 Gerstenhaber manages assets totaling $900 million.\par \par \uc2 Mike Moran, senior emerging market strategist at Standard Chartered in New York, thinks much of the cyclical rally in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is finished, which should make 2010 a tougher year.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to be less directional. We're going to see increased volatility," Moran said.\par \par \uc2 Carry trades thrive in an environment of low volatility, a period when there is diminished risk aversion and the global economy is in the midst of an expansion.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also say given the global downturn, some countries have engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that complicates investing in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 This is part of what makes Brazil a little less alluring despite strong economic fundamentals, analysts said. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, a move aimed at preserving its export competitiveness.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also a threat in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Southeast Asia, and specifically China. Beijing has kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially weak against the dollar, prompting U.S. legislators to threaten tariff sanctions if China doesn't revalue the yuan.\par \par \uc2 MOST EM PORTFOLIOS UNHEDGED\par \par \uc2 Despite these challenges, most emerging market portfolios remained largely unhedged, suggesting a belief that growth will remain strong in the region.\par \par \uc2 "There's certainly a positive correlation between equities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Gavin Francis, head of client management at Pareto Investment Management in London. Pareto is one of the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  managers, with assets totaling $45 billion.\par \par \uc2 "You certainly remove that correlation and you're giving up the gain when you hedge. This whole question of hedging comes down to whether the return is paying you enough for the extra fear that you face in the long term," he added.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered's Moran believes it's impractical to be completely exposed to the vagaries of emerging market investing. "My sense is that 2010 is still a tremendous unknown in terms of policy and the global economy and putting no hedges on your EM portfolios is a punchy move."\par \par \uc2 Overall, the outlook for emerging markets remains strong and hedge funds have kept their faith in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The prospect for growth is a lot stronger in some of these countries, said Aston Chan, portfolio manager at GLC, a London-based macro hedge fund with assets under management of about $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 In that instance, high growth may once again increase the yield advantage of these markets. "The rate-hiking cycle in Brazil and Mexico is going to be much earlier than in G10 countries, except Australia, and this is going increase these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' carry advantage," Chan said. (Reporting by Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss; Editing by Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /EMERGINGMARKETS (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|Z|J|UKI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100330e63u0017v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
