\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Foreign Reserves End 2009 At Near-Record $90.84B\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2010\par 16:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's foreign reserves dipped slightly last week to $90.84 billion, ending 2009 sharply higher than they began after the government cashed in on a major oil hedging program and the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted steady gains in recent months.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico said Tuesday that international reserves slipped by $93 million last week due to mostly unspecified \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reserves were $5.4 billion higher than at the end of 2008, however, and finished 2009 just shy of their historic record of $90.93 billion set Dec. 24.\par \par \uc2 The recent accumulation of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's coffers was possible, in part, thanks to the steady appreciation of Mexico's peso during the past six months.\par \par \uc2 Increasing stability in the peso after a volatile first half of the year allowed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at the end of September, to suspend its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 Between October 2008 and September 2009, the bank auctioned off about $31 billion in the market. Foreign reserves bottomed out at $72.6 billion in August.\par \par \uc2 But with the peso recovering to around MXN13 per dollar at the end of the year from an all-time low of MXN15.60 in March, and oil prices recovering, Mexico's reserves began to be replenished.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to offer $250 million on days when the peso is 2% weaker against the dollar, but in the last three months it has sold only $511 million.\par \par \uc2 Also boosting reserves was the federal government's deposit of about $5 billion last month as the Finance Ministry cashed in on put options purchased in 2008 to protect the budget from fluctuations in the price of Mexican oil.\par \par \uc2 The government hedged much of 2009's oil exports at $70 a barrel when prices soared to record levels during 2008. The government netted about $3.5 billion through the hedges.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso appeared set to continue its New Year rally against the dollar Tuesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was recently quoted in Mexico City trading at MXN12.8085 to the dollar, compared with MXN12.8885 at Monday's close.\par \par \uc2 -By Paul Kiernan, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255)5980-5178, paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 05-01-10 1607GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100105e6150012y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
