\par\par \b \uc2 Reuters Summit-UPDATE 1-Mexico could keep shrinking until 2010\b0\par\par\uc2 380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2009\par 18:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For other news from the Reuters Latin American Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment09?PID=500"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment09?PID=500}}}\uc2 )  (Adds Carstens quotes, background)\par \par \uc2  By Jason Lange\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, May 8 (Reuters) - Mexico's economy will shrink in the third quarter from a year earlier and might not grow again until the first quarter of 2010, Finance Minister Agustin Carstens told Reuters on Friday.\par \par \uc2  Speaking at the Reuters Latin American Investment Summit in Mexico City, Carstens said Mexico's recession was extending longer than previously expected. The country's economy is in freefall because exports to the United States have collapsed.\par \par \uc2  "The two hardest quarters will be the first and second of this year and afterward the GDP will continue to be negative in annual terms," Carstens said. He added that there will be no growth "until the fourth (quarter of this year) or maybe the first quarter of next year."\par \par \uc2  Carstens also said the exchange market for Mexico's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is working "rather well" since the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began selling dollars from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in daily auctions and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  more directly.\par \par \uc2  "The truth is I think the market is working quite well," Carstens said. "What we are interested in is having orderly markets where there's volume and liquidity."\par \par \uc2  The minister, who sits on a commission that decides on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declined to say whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  would halt its daily dollar sales to boost the peso.\par \par \uc2  The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged last year as the economy slipped and investors retreated from emerging markets. But it has strengthened to nearly 13 to the dollar in recent days.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's economy, which relies heavily on U.S. demand, contracted 1.6 percent in the first quarter from a year earlier and a much steeper drop is expected in the period between April and June.\par \par \uc2  The swine flu outbreak that killed dozens of people in Mexico and shut down the economy for five days is expected to shave up to half a percentage point from gross domestic product in 2009.\par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Luis Rojas Mena and Alistair Bell; Editing by Dan Grebler)      \par \par \uc2 LATAM-SUMMIT/CARSTENS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090511e558001we\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
