\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-LatAm falls as US GDP fans recession fears\b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2009\par 00:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * LatAm stocks fall as U.S. GDP suggests deeper recession\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , peso weakens past 15/dlr\par \par \uc2 * Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts rates by 100 bps\par \par \uc2 * Brazil Bovespa briefly erases 2009 gains, closes flat\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Feb 27 (Reuters) - Latin American financial markets fell on Friday after a worse-than-expected U.S. GDP report stoked fears of a deeper and longer global recession that could choke the region's already weakening economic performance.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's markets were among the most hurt due to their heavy reliance on the U.S. economy. While Mexican stocks sank to four-month lows, the peso closed above 15 per dollar for the first time, despite a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The MSCI stock index for Latin America <.MILA00000PUS> fell 1.27 percent to its lowest level in nearly three months. On Wall Street, the S&P 500 <.SPX> closed at 12-year lows, also pressured by news that the U.S. government is taking a large stake in \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's common shares, and possibly in other banks.\par \par \uc2 "This is contaminating investor sentiment, even as emerging economies still post a better economic performance," said Ricardo Tadeu Martins, a research analyst with Planner brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark Bovespa index <.BVSP> erased all of its 2009 gains in the worst moment of the session, but bargain hunters cushioned the market's fall, causing the index to close flat.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's IPC <.MXX> lost 1.63 percent, while Chile's blue-chip IPSA index <.IPSA> slid 1.92 percent.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also weakened on worries that a prolonged world recession may further reduce the dollar inflows from exports.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=><MEX01> lost 1.12 percent to 15.099 per dollar at the government's 1:30 p.m. (1930GMT) final reference rate, even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with direct sales of dollars to banks. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank further to 15.25 later on the day.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> weakened 1.1 percent to 2.37 per dollar while the Chilean peso <CLP=CL> ended 0.18 percent lower at 596.80.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the Colombian peso <COP=RR> strengthened 0.21 percent to 2,554.0 per dollar before the central decision to cut interest rates by 100 basis points, larger than the 50 basis points expected by most economists.\par \par \uc2 The decision could be supportive for the peso next week, as it may be seen as a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is acting to protect the economy, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' senior economist Alberto Ramos wrote in a report.\par \par \uc2 "Admittedly, the current financial conditions are extremely tight for the very weak cyclical position of the economy and that is perhaps one of the key drivers for the recent weakness of the Colombian peso," he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Latin American market prices from Reuters          \par Stock indexes                       Latest        % change\par ----------------------------------------------------------\par MSCI LatAm <.MILA00000PUS>        1,962.50           -1.27\par Brazil Bovespa     <.BVSP>       38,183.31            0.01\par Mexico IPC          <.MXX>       17,752.18           -1.63\par Chile IPSA         <.IPSA>        2,468.63           -1.92\par Chile IGPA         <.IGPA>       11,879.64           -1.47\par Argentina M