\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Mexico's Peso Closes At Record Low 14.96/Dlr\b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2009\par 22:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with additional comments from traders and government officials)\par \par \uc2 By Peter Millard\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's peso closed at a record low on Thursday as concerns about the length of the global downturn hurt the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Mexico City the peso was quoted as closing at 14.96 to the U.S. dollar, sharply weaker than Wednesday's close of MXN14.87. Strong demand from corporate clients who need to make payments to foreign suppliers or pay down foreign debt before the end of the month put pressure on the peso.\par \par \uc2 On its Web site, the Bank of Mexico said the peso hit an intraday record low of 14.9975 to the dollar on Thursday before recovering some ground ahead of the close. One \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said there were some trades on Thursday that broke the MXN15.00 psychological barrier before recovering slightly.\par \par \uc2 "If it stays at this level or weakens more on Friday we could see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the trader.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market with dollar sales to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. The Bank of Mexico started using discretionary dollar sales earlier this month to combat volatility in the exchange market and curtail speculation against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $555 million last week in the exchange market to fight volatility. Mexico's foreign reserves have fallen around $4.5 billion since end-2008, partly due to heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have mentioned the drop in foreign reserves as a source of concern.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Mexico Deputy Gov. Guillermo Guemez said Thursday that he expects the country's foreign reserves to end the year at levels similar to those observed now. The Bank of Mexico has spent about $18.5 billion in reserves since last October to support the peso during periods of high volatility.\par \par \uc2 The gloomy outlook for the U.S. economy has hurt Mexican financial markets because Mexico's economy is heavily dependent on exports to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economic activity fell 2.4% in December from the year-ago month, with declines in industry and services offset by higher agricultural production.\par \par \uc2 Mexican Deputy Finance Minister Alejandro Werner said Thursday the government expects the economy to contract this year due to the global financial crisis that has eroded demand for Mexican exports, slowed foreign investments and eroded remittances from Mexicans working abroad.\par \par \uc2 "All of this will result in a decrease in economic activity," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Peter Millard, Dow Jones Newswires; 52-55-5001-5724; peter.millard@dowjones.com [ 26-02-09 2122GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090226e52q0015d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
