\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  defends peso amid global rout\b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2009\par 16:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds closing stock prices, trader comment)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Feb 23 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso slipped to a another record low on Monday, spurring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars directly to brokers for the second session in a row in a bid to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide.\par \par \uc2 The peso was hammered by uncertainty about U.S. government efforts to shore up beleaguered banks, which pushed investors to dump risky assets like equities and emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mexican stocks sank to a four-month low.\par \par \uc2 "The whole world is taking refuge in the dollar," said Raul Garduno, head of Interacciones brokerage in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 The peso <MXN=><MEX01> weakened 0.82 percent to 14.9225 per dollar at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's final 1:30 p.m. (1930GMT) reference, its weakest official close since new pesos were introduced in 1993.\par \par \uc2 Later in the session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began calling brokers to directly offer dollars, traders said, helping pull the peso sharply back up by about 10 centavos before it slipped again to trade around 14.93 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began a series of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in more than a decade to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Monday, investors initially welcomed reports the government could convert an earlier investment in \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  into a big common stock holding, but enthusiasm faded as investors worried U.S. banks would need even more aid.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. recession has choked off orders for Mexico's exports and the local economy is expected to shrink by more than 1 percent this year. (For a related story, click on [nN23362328])\par \par \uc2 Worries about the impact in Mexico have knocked off about one-third of the peso's value against the dollar since August.\par \par \uc2 The peso was also undermined by a sell-off in peso-denominated bonds as investors bet the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not lower borrowing costs as quickly as expected due to inflationary pressures from the battered peso.\par \par \uc2 The government's benchmark 10-year peso bond <MX10YT=RR> fell 1.662 of a point in price, pushing its yield up 25 basis points to 8.58 percent, its highest since early December.\par \par \uc2 Further pressuring the peso was speculation that \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  would have to unload its Mexican unit Banamex to get cash.\par \par \uc2 "This would weigh heavily on the Mexican peso because if it's a local buyer they would end up having to buy dollars to pay back Citibank," said Nick Chamie, head of emerging market research at RBC in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 In stock trading, the IPC stock index <.MXX> sank 2.01 percent to 17,956.40 points, its lowest close since Oct. 27.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 America Movil\uc2  , Latin America's biggest cell phone operator, shed 4.08 percent to 19.05 pesos.\par \par \uc2 Cemex\uc2  , the top U.S. cement supplier, lost 4.92 percent to 8.50 pesos. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle, Robert Campbell, Noe Torres and Lorena Segura; editing by Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MEXICO/ (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090223e52n0017c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw17