\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Stocks Fall On US Losses; Peso Closes At Record Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2009\par 22:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexican stocks fell for a fifth consecutive session to their lowest close since October, bonds were hammered and the peso closed at a record low against the dollar Monday as a tailspin in U.S. markets fed risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 The local stock market's benchmark IPC index of most-traded issues fell 2%, or 367.83 points, to 17,956.40. Volume was light, with 167.7 million shares traded for a value of 2.87 billion pesos ($192.4 million).\par \par \uc2 Carlos Gonzalez, an equity analyst at Mexico City's Ixe brokerage, said that although the local market's fundamentals have deteriorated, its recent losses reflect more uncertainty than anything else.\par \par \uc2 "I don't see a massive exit from the market; it's just that people aren't buying," Gonzalez said, adding that the trend probably won't change until more is known about the U.S. government's plan to rescue that country's banks.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. government said Monday it will remain "firmly behind the banking system during this period of financial strain." The statement came amid reports that struggling banking giant \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  is in talks with federal officials that could result in the federal government expanding its ownership of the bank.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, which the IPC has been closely following, ended 3.4% lower.\par \par \uc2 Wireless operator and IPC heavyweight \uc2 America Movil\uc2  (AMX) L shares plunged 4.1% to MXN19.05, retailer \uc2 Wal-Mart de Mexico\uc2  (WMMVY) V shares slipped 1.3% to MXN29.10, copper miner Grupo Mexico (GMEXICO.MX) B shares fell 0.3% to MXN8.87, and fixed-line phone company Telmex (TMX) L shares ended 0.9% lower at MXN10.63.\par \par \uc2 Cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  (CX) CPO shares slid 4.9% to MXN8.50 after Fitch Ratings further downgraded the company's credit ratings, citing concerns about its high level of debt in the ongoing economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 Prices also fell sharply in the secondary market for local government debt, pushing the yield on 10-year bonds due 2017 up 27 basis points to 8.57% and the yield on 20-year bonds due 2024 up 40 basis points to 9.07%.\par \par \uc2 Bonds came under pressure last week after the Bank of Mexico cut the overnight rate by 25 basis points to 7.5%, when most market participants were expecting a 50-basis-point cut.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso, meanwhile, closed at a record low against the dollar as weak local stocks and global risk aversion pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearer to the psychological barrier of MXN15 to the dollar and prompting the Bank of Mexico to sell dollars directly in the market.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted in Mexico City closing at MXN14.95 to the dollar, compared with MXN14.7645 at the close Friday.\par \par \uc2 A local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said the peso appears ready to break the MXN15 mark, as direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past two sessions have done little to curb its depreciation.\par \par \uc2 "The outlook is nothing positive," the trader said, adding that odds are "remote" that the peso could reverse its weakening trend seen in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold an undisclosed amount of dollars into the exchange market Monday afternoon, the trader said, temporarily causing the peso to strengthen to MXN14.83 from MXN14.93 - gains it quickly gave up.\par \par \uc2 "Given the market's reaction, I don't feel like there was a large amount (of dollars) involved," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico started using discretionary dollar sales earlier this month to combat volatility in the exchange market and curtail speculation against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It discloses the amount of dollars it sells in a weekly report on foreign reserves, published each Tuesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sold dollars directly in the market last Friday, in addition to $400 million sold in its scheduled daily auctions.\par \par \uc2 -By Paul Kiernan, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255)5001-5726, paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 23-02-09 2141GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852 5047   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090223e52n000r2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
