\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Mexico economy contracts,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  cuts rates\b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2009\par 17:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mexico economy shrinks at 1.6 percent clip in Q4\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts rates by 25 basis points\par \par \uc2 * Peso falls again, bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars\par \par \uc2 By Jason Lange\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy contracted late last year as the U.S. recession tore into demand for exports, data showed Friday, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disappointed investors by cutting interest rates less than expected.\par \par \uc2 The peso fell sharply following the rate decision and on comments by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it was worried about a tumble in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has lost about 30 percent of its value since last August due to the world economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 The economy shrank at a 1.6 percent annual clip in the fourth quarter, meaning Mexico is firmly on the path toward recession. The government expects the economy will also contract during the first and second quarters of this year, and some private sector analysts are more pessimistic.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. recession, triggered by a collapse in the American housing market, has choked off orders for everything from cars to refrigerators made in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its key interest rate by 25 basis points to 7.50 percent to try to soften the blow of an economic downturn unseen in the country since the Tequila Crisis of the mid-1990s.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts expected a 50 basis point reduction.\par \par \uc2 The bank warned it was increasingly concerned about the recent deterioration of the peso which could fuel inflation.\par \par \uc2 "Strong financial turbulence seen recently represents a risk for inflation expectations," the bank said in its monthly statement.\par \par \uc2 Markets jumped on that comment, as well as the size of the rate cut which was seen as timid, sending the peso downward.\par \par \uc2 "It has raised the specter in the market of whether they are trying to defend a range. That is very dangerous because it invites attacks," said \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economist Gray Newman.\par \par \uc2 The peso's depreciation prompted the bank to sell dollars directly to dealers again on Friday to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was the latest of a series of measures to defend the peso that have had little success.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is contributing to the very volatility that they are trying to avoid," Newman said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> <MEX01> was down 1.22 percent to 14.80 per dollar on Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 A weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  means many imports will become more expensive, raising fears of an inflation bump.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economy grew a tepid 1.3 percent in 2008, slowing sharply at the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased monetary policy last month for the first time in nearly three years, cutting its benchmark rate by 50 basis points. Investors see it trimming its key rate to as low as 6.0 percent this year to boost growth. (Additional reporting by Noel Randewich, Luis Rojas Mena and Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|Table|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090223e52k00194\par }\page {\par\fs20{\