\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico Peso Closes At New Record Low Vs Dlr After Rate Cut\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Parks   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2009\par 21:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's peso closed at a record low versus the U.S. dollar Friday, as investors reacted negatively to a lower-than-expected interest-rate cut by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted in Mexico City closing at MXN14.7645 to the dollar, compared to an intraday low of MXN14.9850, and Thursday's close of MXN14.6100.\par \par \uc2 The peso weakened after the Bank of Mexico announced its decision at 10:00 a.m. EST to cut the benchmark overnight rate by 25 basis points to 7.5%. The market had expected a 50-basis-point cut to boost Mexico's sputtering economy.\par \par \uc2 The peso's slide prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell $400 million at two scheduled auctions Friday and to sell an undisclosed amount of dollars directly to trading desks.\par \par \uc2 "We view this weakness as indicative of concern over recession in Mexico and the economy's links to the contracting U.S. economy; sentiment on Mexico is uniformly negative. We doubt that the prospect of sharply lower Mexican interest rates has been a key driver of peso weakness," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said in a report.\par \par \uc2 While recognizing that the downside risks to the economy have worsened, the Bank of Mexico said in its communique Friday that it is concerned recent volatility in financial markets could jeopardize the bank's inflation target.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico has forecast the annual inflation rate ending the year under 4%, and reaching its 3% target by the end of 2010.\par \par \uc2 The peso has fallen 6.4% against the dollar this year, after dropping 21% during 2008, as investors fear Mexico will join the U.S. in a deep recession. A weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens to feed inflation, which is still more than double the target.\par \par \uc2 "If indeed policy is now being driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns, then Bank of Mexico will need to show resolve to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If market perception in regards to Mexican risk does not improve, this cannot be done without substantial use of reserves. The market may well test Bank of Mexico's resolve," RBS Greenwich Capital said in a report.\par \par \uc2 Prior to Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent nearly $17.92 billion in foreign reserves, which stood at $81.56 billion on Feb. 13, since last October to support the peso.\par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Guillermo Ortiz said last week that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue if needed to curtail volatility, but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't targeting a specific exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, Mexican government bonds were hit by selling, pushing yields sharply higher. The yield on 10-year bonds due 2017 rose 32 basis points to 8.25%, while the yield on 20-year bonds due 2027 spiked 43 basis points to 8.65%.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark IPC stock index was down 1.8% less than an hour from the close, tracking losses on Wall Street as investors fretted over the possibility the U.S. government might be forced to nationalize some of the country's biggest banks.\par \par \uc2 -By Ken Parks, Dow Jones Newswires; 52-55-5001-5723; ken.parks@dowjones.com [ 20-02-09 2050GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090220e52k000vk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
