\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Latin America cenbanks need to cut rates faster\b0\par\par\uc2 By Simon Gardner   \par \uc2 882 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2009\par 19:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Feb 20 (Reuters) - Latin America's central banks are cutting interest rates to save their economies from recession, but some are not moving quickly enough and risk being overwhelmed as the global crisis bites deep.\par \par \uc2 Central banks across the region started cutting benchmark lending rates this year as economic activity, industrial output and retail sales sank.\par \par \uc2 Mexico, Chile and Peru have also expanded government spending, hoping that fiscal stimulus plans, combined with cheaper credit for consumers and businesses, will do the trick.\par \par \uc2 Chile has led the charge on monetary policy, slashing 2.5 percentage points off its monetary policy rate earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 But Brazil and Mexico have less room to maneuver and their central bankers are famously cautious, determined to prevent a return to the hyperinflation that triggered a string of financial crises in the late 20th century.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is above target in both countries, so policymakers worry sharp rate declines could further weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, causing new inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 Still, they are drawing criticism from some analysts who say the region's two biggest economies need a dramatic jolt now rather than gradual relief.\par \par \uc2 "It is useless to distribute the monetary relaxation along the whole year when the strongest effect from the external recession is going to be felt in the first three months of the year," said Alfredo Coutino, senior Latin America economist for \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Economy.com.\par \par \uc2 "You have to be aggressive," he said. "You need to apply the antibiotic immediately. You don't need to dosify over 12 months like some banks are thinking in the case of Mexico ... I don't know why they are waiting. What for?"\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely expected to cut rates by a further half a percentage point on Friday, but it was more cautious, lowering the benchmark rate by just one-quarter of a point.\par \par \uc2 Central banks have room for further rate cuts because inflationary pressures will likely continue to ease, given steep falls in international commodity, food and fuel prices and sagging consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 "All the major central banks in the region will ultimately be cutting rates, just because the disinflationary implication of the downturn will dominate the short-run inflationary effect of the depreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Rodrigo Valdes, chief Latin America economist for Barclays Capital Research in New York.\par \par \uc2 RECESSION\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economy is falling into recession and inflationary pressures have begun to ease but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has to contend with the impact of the falling peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pummeled since late last year by worries about deepening recession in the United States, which buys around 80 percent of Mexico's exports, the peso <.MXN><MEX01> hit record lows on Friday despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government has turned to budget deficit spending and loan guarantees for corporate borrowers to counter a credit crunch and economic slowdown, it has limited room for more fiscal stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is also in a tight corner and has had to put a temporary freeze on a quarter of its 2009 budget as the economy slows, tax revenues fall and companies lay off workers. The unemployment rate jumped to 8.2 percent in January as hundreds of thousands of people lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 "Probably in a couple of countries we may see more (fiscal stimulus), but the problem is there is not much financing to do more fiscal packages," Valdes said.\par \par \uc2 "So more likely what we will see is some countries will have to probably be more careful with their spending because their fiscal deficit will be larger -- including Brazil, Colombia, Mexico most likely."\par \par \uc2 Chile, long touted as Latin America's most stable economy for its fiscal prudence, has cut its benchmark rate, which was at a 10-year high last year of 8.25 percent by 3.5 percentage points so far this year, hand in hand with a $4 billion government fiscal stimulus plan.\par \par \uc2 But while a clutch of Chilean banks have started to pass on some of those cuts to customers, others are lagging and many consumers won't feel the benefits for months.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark Selic lending rate is still at a whopping 12.75 percent even after a 100 basis point cut last month that was its first reduction in 16 months.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is signaling there will be more cuts ahead, as Brazil's growth has slowed sharply since the global credit crunch bit deep in September, hammering output and prompting layoffs.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities in Chile and Brazil have realized that, contrary to their original expectations, this is a very serious global economic meltdown," said Sebastian Edwards, professor of international economics at the University of California, Los Angeles' Anderson School of Management.\par \par \uc2 "Both Brazil and Chile had very high interest rates at the onset of the crisis. Bringing them down aggressively is the appropriate thing to do, and will help better weather the crisis by providing additional liquidity." (With reporting by Ariel Miranda in Santiago; Editing by Kieran Murray and Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/LATINAMERICA-CENBANKS (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|BNX|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090220e52k001oq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
