\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Peso Weakens To New Closing Low Vs Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2009\par 21:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's peso lost ground against the dollar Tuesday, slipping to a new closing low as investor risk aversion and economic worries continued to hit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted in Mexico City at MXN14.6950 around 3:20 p.m. EST, compared with MXN14.6330 in early trade and MXN14.5465 at the close on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cut-off reference rate at 2:30 p.m. EST was MXN14.67, although participants pushed the peso briefly above MXN14.73 in a flurry of trading near the close.\par \par \uc2 "Market sentiment remains negative, focusing on the question of risk, also watching the Dow," said a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader.\par \par \uc2 Investor concerns about declines in economic activity, and continued worries about the financial sector, weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks Tuesday. The local market's IPC stock index was down 3%, tracking the Dow Jones Industrial Average which was 2.9% lower.\par \par \uc2 The peso's depreciation wasn't enough to trigger dollar sales at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily auctions, in which the bank offers $400 million at a rate 2% above the previous session's rate.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Clyde Wardle said in a report that the peso is the most vulnerable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Latin America given Mexico's close economic ties to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "While we believe that the bulk of the peso's loss against the U.S. dollar has already taken place, we see further room for depreciation, associated with our expectations of worsening conditions both in the U.S. and Mexico," wrote Wardle, who sees the peso ending the year around MXN15.00.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the exchange market, selling $1.06 billion in addition to its scheduled auctions as volatility pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to new lows.\par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Guillermo Ortiz said last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue if needed to curtail volatility, but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't targeting a specific exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5001 5727; anthony.harrup@dowjones.com [ 17-02-09 2033GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090217e52h000vr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
