\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's peso to keep slipping despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael O'Boyle   \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2009\par 20:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Feb 12 (Reuters) - A landmark \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the battered peso is slowing its decline, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen weakening further as Mexico slides into recession.\par \par \uc2 The bank startled markets last week by making direct U.S. dollar sales to brokers for the first time in more than a decade to prop up the sickly peso <MXN> <MEX01> after it hit a historic low of 14.70 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The bank has ample reserves to play with but market players see the peso weakening even further, testing the bank's resolve over how low it is willing to see it go.\par \par \uc2 Worries that U.S. efforts to stabilize the economy and shore up banks may not work knocked the peso back down this week to near its record lows.\par \par \uc2 With Mexico's exports in a tailspin, migrants in the United States sending home less cash, and state coffers hurting from declining oil output and depressed crude prices, analysts see little to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 "All the factors that justify a depreciation of the peso are operating," said \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's chief Mexico economist Alfredo Thorne, who expects the peso to soon break past 15 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Last week's $1-billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped pull the peso back by more than 50 centavos from 14.70 per dollar, its weakest since new pesos were introduced in 1993 and well off its 11-per-dollar level of the last few years. But nerves over the U.S. bank bailout sent it back above 14.60 by Thursday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Gov. Guillermo Ortiz said on Wednesday he expected more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as markets remain volatile.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers are sitting on a hard-earned war chest of $82 billion in foreign reserves built up from oil exports. Analysts say that is plenty, for now, to keep up the fight to at least make sure the peso's slide is less volatile.\par \par \uc2 "The mystery is at what level the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be comfortable with the exchange rate," said Daniela Blancas, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 AGAINST THE WALL\par \par \uc2 Besides its reserves, Mexico has access to a $30 billion swap line with the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 It should also be able to replenish reserves with around $12 billion, according to analysts, from an oil price hedge that guarantees at least $70 per barrel for local crude. Mexico can also tap big credit lines from multilateral institutions.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has worked hard to establish its global credibility as a prudent and transparent \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the so-called "Tequila Crisis" devasted the country's finances in the mid-1990s.\par \par \uc2 But the peso's loss of a quarter of its value since October, has put the bank against a wall as it fields pressure to slash borrowing costs to boost the economy.\par \par \uc2 While President Felipe Calderon and Mexican companies want lower interest rates, policymakers worry the peso's freefall is pushing up import costs and fueling already high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Cutting borrowing costs could further undermine the peso as lower interest rates would make Mexico's debt less attractive.\par \par \uc2 To stabilize the peso the bank turned to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  after regular dollar auctions started in October failed to stem the rout and invited speculation due to their predictability.\par \par \uc2 Some see the peso catching a break if U.S. stimulus plans work. BBVA Bancomer sees it rebounding to as strong as 12.50 per dollar by the end of 2009 if a U.S. rebound emboldens investors to return to Mexican debt.\par \par \uc2 Others say hopes for a quick U.S. recovery are unfounded and a deep downturn could hobble Mexico through 2010. Moreover, Mexico's oil price hedge will be exhausted by then and it may be still grappling with low crude prices and declining output.\par \par \uc2 "If the world does not recover by the second part of the year, that is what will really make the market nervous," said Salvador Orozco, Santander's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-PESO/ (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090215e52c000h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
