\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 : Will Continue To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Mkt\b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2009\par 05:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts lead, adds further comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor)\par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Bank of Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz said Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if there is excessive volatility" but isn't targeting a specific exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "We are not looking for a certain level of exchange rate. We certainly maintain our belief in the benefits of a floating exchange rate," he told reporters at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 Ortiz said the depreciation of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar has become pronounced around the world.\par \par \uc2 "The peso is no exception and the volatility has increased," he said.\par \par \uc2 He added that trying to predict the future path of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a very complicated matter.\par \par \uc2 "My own view is that the fundamentals in Mexico are solid," he said, citing the banking system and debt indicators.\par \par \uc2 The peso has been under pressure in recent weeks, affected by falling expectations for economic growth, lower crude oil prices and the beginning of a monetary easing cycle.\par \par \uc2 Ortiz also said Mexico is projecting a current account deficit of 2.4% of gross domestic product for 2009, and expects January industrial production output to fall by 30% to 40%.\par \par \uc2 "In Mexico, we are expecting in January a fall of around 30%-40%," Ortiz said.\par \par \uc2 Late last month, the Bank of Mexico said it expects the nation's economy to contract between 0.8% and 1.8% this year due to the recession in the U.S. Mexico's economy grew an estimated 1.5% in 2008. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  previously forecast growth of 0.5%-1.5% for 2009.\par \par \uc2 Ortiz said the country's international reserves will be substantially higher at the year-end, while inflation should remain within expectations.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier said consumer-price inflation would end the year below 4%.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico now sees annual inflation averaging 5.75% to 6.25% in the first quarter, up from its previous forecast of 5.25% to 5.75%. In the second quarter, annual inflation will average 5.25% to 5.75%, up from 4.5% to 5.0%.\par \par \uc2 Asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut interest rates further, Ortiz reiterated that monetary actions will depend on many factors.\par \par \uc2 Ortiz earlier said future monetary policy actions hinge on "inflation following the trajectory" forecast by the Bank of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 16, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed its overnight rate to 7.75% from 8.25% and more cuts are expected.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Mexico is expected to cut the overnight rate to 6.25% by the end of the year, according to the latest market survey by \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . (C) unit Banamex, published in late January.\par \par \uc2 -By Elffie Chew, Dow Jones Newswires; (603) 2026 1233; elffie.chew@dowjones.com [ 11-02-09 0424GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090211e52b0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
