\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-LatAm sinks on US bank bailout frustration\b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2009\par 23:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * LatAm stocks drop 2.8 pct on US banking plan frustration\par \par \uc2  * Emerging debt returns now negative for the year\par \par \uc2  * Rating downgrade fears weigh on Mexico's peso\par \par \uc2  By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 10 (Reuters) - Latin American financial markets dropped on Tuesday as disappointment over a U.S. bank bailout plan increased fears that developed economies will not recover any time soon, dragging a larger number of emerging countries into recession.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's assets were the hardest hit because investors fear the country's strong links to the ailing U.S. economy may turn it into a major casualty of the global crisis.\par \par \uc2  Latin American stocks fell 2.81 percent on the MSCI index <.MILA00000PUS> after U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner unveiled a reworked plan to shore up the financial sector without all the details sought by investors.\par \par \uc2  "The lack of a clear plan, and (lack of) sentiment that authorities are in control, kept the market depressed in the aftermath of the secretary's speech," Kathryn G. Rooney, senior emerging-market strategist at Bulltick Capital Markets, said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's IPC stock index <.MXX> plunged 3.46 percent, the largest loss in the region, while Brazil's Bovespa index <.BVSP> declined 2.12 percent and Argentina's MerVal <.MERV> dropped 2.71 percent.\par \par \uc2  Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted losses, with the Brazilian real <BRBY> weakening 2 percent to 2.284 per dollar after five straight sessions of gains.\par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso <MXN=> ended 1.97 percent weaker at 14.4825 per dollar after a four-day winning streak which started when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to sell dollars directly to banks last week.\par \par \uc2  The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also pressured by concerns that the country's sovereign ratings could be downgraded soon, further reducing investors' appetite for domestic assets.\par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Fitch analyst Shelly Shetty said the ratings agency is carefully monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , among other issues, to decide whether to downgrade the country. For details, see [ID:nN09524563].\par \par \uc2  Fitch is currently the only among the three main ratings agencies to have Mexico's "BBB+" rating on a negative outlook.\par \par \uc2  Emerging-market bond prices declined 0.79 percent in average, erasing all of their gains for the year-to-date, according to the \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  EMBI+ index <11EMJ>.\par \par \uc2  Yield spreads between emerging debt and U.S. Treasuries widened 24 basis points to 648 basis points, the EMBI+ showed.                                      \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING/|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090213e52a0031x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
