\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  Fin Min:Ctrl Bk To Continue  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Mkt\b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2009\par 16:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with additional information from the Finance Minister, Mexican peso quote)\par \par \uc2 By Claudia Assis\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Mexican Finance Minister Agustin Carstens said Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue with unscheduled \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the peso.\par \par \uc2 The goal with the discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  - in addition to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular, $400 million auctions - is to keep an "orderly market," Carstens said.\par \par \uc2 The Banxico on Wednesday sold U.S. dollars directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, its first unscheduled \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly four months, to staunch the Mexican peso's free-fall in the past week.\par \par \uc2 Carstens on Friday declined to disclose how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent, saying the answer lies with the Banxico. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide threatens to increase inflation and damage the government's finances.\par \par \uc2 The unscheduled \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue whenever the situation calls for them, Carstens told reporters on the sidelines of a conference on the Mexican housing industry.\par \par \uc2 The action Wednesday was necessary because some market participants took the opportunity to make profits from the weaker peso, he told the audience earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso was quoted in Mexico City Friday morning at MXN14.17 versus the dollar, compared to Thursday close of MXN14.28. The peso closed at a record low of MXN14.57 Tuesday, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $400 million in two scheduled auctions. Some market participants see the peso around MXN14.90 to the dollar in the second or third quarter of this year.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hover around $80 billion and the Banxico has "sufficient arsenal" to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "which may put a cap on near-term (Mexican peso) weakness," \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  said in a report late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In his presentation to the investors, Carstens reiterated the Finance Ministry's call for flat 2009 gross domestic product for Mexico or a contraction of 1%. That contrasts with Banxico's current forecast of a contraction between 0.8% and 1.8%.\par \par \uc2 Growth remains "a main challenge" for Mexico, Carstens said. "The Mexican economy will not be spared this world tsunami."\par \par \uc2 But Mexico's economy will decline at a slower pace than the U.S., he added. In addition, the country is better prepared to weather the crisis, with no "financing problems in sight," Carstens said.\par \par \uc2 Carstens quipped that exactly one year ago he predicted that Mexico would only catch a "small cold" from the crisis brewing in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 A year later, Mexico is still doing better than the U.S., but as the richer neighbor "caught something worse, what we've got was worse," he said. "As much as we want to move forward it will take some time" for the U.S. to resolve its problems. Compounding Mexico's trouble is the decrease in the price of oil and other commodities, he said.\par \par \uc2 Carstens also said remittances - money that Mexicans abroad send home - will likely be lower even in real terms, taking into consideration the peso's devaluation.\par \par \uc2 What is happening in the U.S. is "of the essence" for Mexico, he said. But unlike past crisis, there's a silver lining, Carstens said.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico is facing this situation from a relative position of health and strength," he said. "We are close to the epicenter geographically but we have not created the crisis."\par \par \uc2 Carstens boasted of Mexico's healthy banking system and public finances, pointing out Mexico's public debt has decreased in GDP terms.\par \par \uc2 The minister said he expects Banxico to keep cutting interest rates. "In 2009 we will see lower interest rates providing support for the economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 He added the government also continues to be committed to the housing sector. "Housing is not only an essential need" but a high employment-creation industry, he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Claudia Assis, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; claudia.assis@dowjones.com.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 06, 2009 10:40 ET (15:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090820e5260mjy4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
