\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  stocks rally, peso firms on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2009\par 23:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Feb 6 (Reuters) - Mexican stocks posted their biggest jump in a month on Friday amid hopes the U.S. government will soon pass an economic stimulus package while the battered peso firmed as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets for the third straight day.\par \par \uc2 The IPC stock index <.MXX> soared 3.55 percent to end at 20,438.13 points -- its biggest one-day percentage gain since Jan. 2. It was led by gains in Latin America's top cellphone provider \uc2 America Movil\uc2  after the firm posted a surprise jump in fourth-quarter profits.\par \par \uc2 The peso <MEX01> <MXN=> firmed 0.39 percent to end at 14.21 per dollar at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's final reference rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars directly to market players and the government vowed to keep defending the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nN06463782]\par \par \uc2 U.S. lawmakers struggled to craft a stimulus package on Friday worth more than $900 billion while expectations grew that the Treasury would announce fresh aid for crippled banks as early as Monday.\par \par \uc2 Any boost for the U.S. economy bodes well for Mexico, which sends around 80 percent of its exports to its northern neighbor.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economy is expected to shrink by more than 1 percent this year as the United States buys less exports, manufacturers lay off workers and consumer spending falls.\par \par \uc2 Traders were skeptical that Friday's jump marked solid gains for Mexican stocks.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't rule out a complete turnaround for the market, given we will keep seeing bad data," said Mauricio Cervantes, a stock trader at Multivalores brokerage in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 U.S. non-farm payrolls fell in January by the most for any month since December 1974, sending the unemployment rate in the world's largest economy up to 7.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 America Movil\uc2  gained 6.1 percent to 22.25 pesos after reporting a 14.5 percent jump in fourth-quarter profits. [ID:nN05395478]\par \par \uc2 The company told analysts during a conference call on Friday that it plans to invest $3 billion to build up its network during 2009 and could add 19 million new subscribers this year. [ID:nN06447354]\par \par \uc2 America Movil\uc2  shares in New York gained 6.48 percent to $31.05\par \par \uc2 Top retailer \uc2 Wal-Mart de Mexico\uc2  <WALMEXV.MX> climbed 3.94 percent to 29.83 pesos after improved sales results.\par \par \uc2 The company, known as Walmex, said on Thursday that sales at stores that had been open for at least 12 months rose 2.7 percent in January, compared with a 0.8 percent decline in December. [ID:nN06363780]\par \par \uc2 Miner Grupo Mexico <GMEXICOB.MX>, the country's biggest copper producer, surged 7.76 percent to 9.86 percent. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MEXICO/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090206e526002mt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
