\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  Peso Gains To MXN14.2830/Dlr;  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2009\par 21:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso closed stronger against the dollar Thursday amid considerably less volatility than in the previous session, as traders reported continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market.\par \par \uc2 The peso was quoted in Mexico City closing at MXN14.2830 to the dollar, compared with MXN14.3805 at the open and MXN14.4650 at Wednesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Several traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market by calling local banks to sell dollars.\par \par \uc2 The peso has weakened substantially since the beginning of the year, and it set a record intraday low Wednesday upon hitting MXN14.70.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's exchange commission, composed of Bank of Mexico and Finance Ministry officials, said Wednesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market by selling an unspecified amount of dollars to boost liquidity and curb volatility.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't confirm Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but noted that the exchange commission implied it might continue selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have said they see the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a sign that the government is growing uncomfortable with a further weakening in the peso, while others think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  merely wants to minimize volatility.\par \par \uc2 Analysts are also divided on where the peso is headed.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a morning note that the peso's recent depreciation is the result of a worsening local economy and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "fair value" is around MXN15.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Bancomer said the peso has been oversold as a result of "uncertainty and credit illiquidity."\par \par \uc2 Either way, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem to have caused market participants to hesitate before speculating against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was relatively stable Thursday after trading in a broad range between MXN14.42 and MXN14.70 in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 But a local trader said that what's more important than the peso's current level is the amount of reserves the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is spending to support it.\par \par \uc2 "We're obviously happy that the peso is back below MXN14.50," the trader said. "But what we have to see is if [the Bank of Mexico] has the ability to keep it there."\par \par \uc2 The bank plans to disclose the cost of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its weekly report on foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 As of Jan. 30, reserves stood at $83.63 billion.\par \par \uc2 -By Paul Kiernan, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255)5001-5726, paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 05-02-09 2046GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1027 1852   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090205e5250014z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
