\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-LatAm assets hurt by further economic fears\b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2008\par 19:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Latin American stocks end mostly lower in volatile trade\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso weakens despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real supported by bold \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 23 (Reuters) - Latin American stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds ended mostly lower on Thursday as persistent economic fears offset some Wall Street gains and a rebound in the price of oil.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil prices settled 1.63 percent higher and rallied as much as 4 percent in after-hours trading, but other commodities fell, with investors still worried that an expected global economic recession will hurt exports and emerging countries.\par \par \uc2 "The slowdown in the G7 will hurt emerging markets through a broad range of channels, some of them self-reinforcing," \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 ' analysts wrote in a research note, citing prospects of falling commodity prices, declining import demand, lower foreign direct investment, and restricted foreign financing.\par \par \uc2 As credit conditions continue to tighten in emerging markets, investors fear some companies might have refinancing problems. Banks are also on investors' radar, since a decline in lending activity might affect their profitability.\par \par \uc2 Bank shares led the Brazilian benchmark Bovespa index <.BVSP> 3.57 percent lower, while Mexico's IPC index <.MXX> lost 5.26 percent. Overall, Latin American shares fell 1.54 percent according to the MSCI stock index for the region <.MILA00000PUS>.\par \par \uc2 But Argentina's MerVal <.MERV> bucked the regional trend, gaining 2.43 percent in a technical correction that followed losses of more than 10 percent on Wednesday, after a government plan to take over the country's private pension funds unnerved investors.\par \par \uc2 Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also weakened on economic fears, but the Brazilian real <BRBY> ended 3.3 percent higher after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily sold dollars on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The bank also unveiled a program to sell up to $50 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to show it has the means to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That puts speculators on alert and shows that, if needed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make use of all the weapons it has," said Vanderlei Arruda, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 Souza Barros\uc2  brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sold $1 billion on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday but that did not stop its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=><MEX01> from weakening 0.7 percent. The peso has lost 6.7 percent of its value in four consecutive sessions of losses.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso <CLP=CL> weakened 1.4 percent to close at its weakest level in four years, hurt by a further decline in the price of copper, the country's main export.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market sovereign bonds remained under pressure, with yield spreads over U.S. Treasuries widening an additional 27 basis points to 829 points, according to the \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  EMBI+ index <11EMJ>.\par \par \uc2 IMF READYING AID FACILITIES\par \par \uc2 Outside of Latin America, other emerging-market governments were also struggling to contain the fallout of the global credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its main deposit rate by 0.5 percentage point to fight against capital outflows, while data showed the country's foreign reserves fell by $15 billion last week as Moscow tries to defend the ruble.\par \par \uc2 Countries without significant foreign reserves such as Iceland, Hungary, Ukraine, Serbia, Turkey and Belarus are reaching out to the International Monetary Fund for financial aid.\par \par \uc2 The IMF also said it is hurrying to approve by early November a liquidity swap facility that would provide liquidity to a select group of well-run emerging economies. For details, see [ID:nN23526514].\par \par \uc2 The facility would allow governments to swap their domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for U.S. dollars. Opening swap lines usually gives central banks more ability to lend to their local commercial banks, easing credit strains.\par \par \uc2 In other news, \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  placed Bulgaria's "BBB+" sovereign credit rating on "CreditWatch negative" on concerns over the country's heightened external vulnerabilities. S&P said it will decide on Bulgaria's ratings by the end of the month, depending on the government response to the crisis [ID:nLN737475]. (Additional reporting by Jenifer Correa in Sao Paulo; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081026e4an000ih\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
