\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico peso plunges 6 percent on global crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2008\par 14:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds detail and background)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Oct 8 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso plunged more than 6 percent against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday as investors, shocked by a burgeoning world credit crisis, fled to safer securities such as U.S. Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 The peso <MEX01> <MXN=> traded at 12.97 per dollar, having earlier lost almost 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also pressured by soaring demand from local investors and companies in need of dollars to meet foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated payments, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Central banks around the world, not including Mexico, cut interest rates in unison on Wednesday to try to limit economic damage from the worst financial crisis in 80 years.\par \par \uc2 In an unscheduled announcement made as New York traders were reaching their offices, the U.S. Federal Reserve cut its key federal funds rate to 1.5 percent from 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, where banks have largely weathered the credit crunch, some traders speculated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso.\par \par \uc2 A further economic slowdown in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be surprising because of the country's strong trade links with the United States, International Monetary Fund chief economist Olivier Blanchard warned in Washington on Wednesday. (Reporting by Noel Randewich and Michael O'Boyle; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-PESO/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081008e4a80013u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppi