\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Markets-LatAm markets roiled, central banks  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2008\par 22:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Selling of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  met by \b \highlight22\uc2 central.bank interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Global coordinated rate cuts give only temporary boost\par \par \uc2 * Sovereign debt spreads widen\par \par \uc2 * Russia and Ukraine halt stock market trading\par \par \uc2 * Iceland credit rating cut 3 notches by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  (Recasts, new throughout)\par \par \uc2 By Daniel Bases\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 8 (Reuters) - Bouts of panic selling whipsawed Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday forcing central banks to uncork \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures that pushed the Mexican and Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  back up from multi-year lows.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  however did not help allay investor fears completely. Emerging market credit default swap spreads widened, indicating investor risk appetite continues to evaporate, although regional stock indexes recovered some ground from their lows.\par \par \uc2 Sovereign debt issues fell sharply and pushed yields on the benchmark Brazil 2040 bond, considered a proxy for the sector, to a three-year high of 7.802 percent on a flight-to-safety move <BRAGLB40=RR>.\par \par \uc2 The yield spread on the benchmark \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus <11EMJ><.JPMEMBIPLUS> widened by 13 basis points to 516 basis points over U.S. Treasuries. Earlier the spread hit 525, its worst level since May 20, 2004.\par \par \uc2 Latin America's distinct interest rate yield advantage was made more glaring by the coordinated interest rate cut by developed markets. The first coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut since the Sept. 11, 2001 attacks was largely dismissed by the investors concerned authorities don't yet have a grip on the global credit crisis. (For related story click on [ID:nHKG77476].)\par \par \uc2 "It is completely panic selling. It is illiquidity compounded by fears of lower growth," said Benito Berber, Latin American strategist at RBS in Stamford, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 "The coordinated rate cut should have made a difference but emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are extremely susceptible to growth concerns and are extremely, extremely illiquid, so that allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adjust very quickly and that prompted actions of central banks," he said.\par \par \uc2 CENTRAL BANKS\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp slide to a 16-year low of more than 14 per dollar earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times on Wednesday in order to prop up the real, which at one point fell more than 6.0 percent in value to its weakest point since August 2005 against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 "It seems that global investors had recently been using the dollar/peso as hedging vehicle, especially today when dollar/real trading was halted in the BM&F after it rose 6.0 percent," wrote Roberto Melzi of \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The announcement of U.S. dollar sales by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  calmed the market and permitted dollar/peso to recover from the 14.05 unprecedented intra-day high."\par \par \uc2 For a time on Wednesday trading of dollar/real futures contracts froze on Brazil's Commodities and Futures Exchange (BM&F) when levels hit limits established by the exchange.\par \par \uc2 After the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regained its footing, rising 0.74 percent to 2.294 per U.S. dollar <BRBY>.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso plunged more than 6.0 percent against the U.S. dollar as investors fled to the relative safety of U.S. Treasuries, which were themselves lower on the day.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MEX01><MXN=> traded down 7.71 percent at 13.16 per dollar, having earlier lost more than 15 percent which brought it to its weakest level in more than a decade of 14.32. (For related stories click on [ID:nN082099] and [ID:nN081356])\par \par \uc2 "The peso has become the whipping boy for emerging markets today," said Gray Newman, senior Latin American economist at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 STOCK MARKET TURBULENCE\par \par \uc2 After an initial sharp downturn in Latin American stocks, losses were trimmed but still left benchmark indices at their weakest points in over two years.\par \par \uc2 "The (interest rate cuts) announced today give you the impression that governments are a little desperate. Monetary policy has only a limited effect, because we have a confidence crisis," said Roberto Padovani, Sao Paulo-based chief economist with WestLB.\par \par \uc2 A surprise increase in pending U.S. home sales for August helped buoy sentiment in U.S. markets, although volatile trade left them down on the day. (For related stories, click on [ID:nN08520388] and [ID:nN08184853])\par \par \uc2 Initially, the MSCI Latin American stock index plunged more than 14 percent to its lowest point since June 2006. By day's end the loss was cut to 5.43 percent <.MILA00000PUS>.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Bovespa stock index <.BVSP> ended down 3.85 percnt at 38,593.4, while Mexico's IPC stock index <.MXX> ended down just 0.98 percent at 20,678.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's global emerging market stock index <.MSCIEF> fell to a three-year low before recovering slightly to end with a better than 7.0 percent decline on the day.\par \par \uc2 Latin America's central banks did not participate in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cuts and in recent months have been raising interest rates to fight rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 The direction for rates in the region could change given global recession fears and the fall in commodity prices which has lowered inflation concerns.\par \par \uc2 ICELAND MELTS, UKRAINE DISOLVES\par \par \uc2 In European emerging markets, Iceland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  collapsed after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned efforts to defend the crown, allowing it to plunge from a two-day attempt to hold it at 131 per euro.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers outside Iceland have been using Reuters matching system, traders said as the local market has stopped functioning smoothly.\par \par \uc2 The system was showing that the Icelandic crown dealt at 340 per euro <EURISK=D3> versus 245 per euro on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service cut Iceland's sovereign credit rating three notches to "A1" from "Aa1" while Fitch Ratings chopped its rating three notches to "BBB-" from "A-".\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's credit default swap spreads, a measure of risk sentiment, deteriorated on Wednesday moving to 1,015 from 899 on Tuesday, according to date provider Markit <UAGV5YUSAC=MP>.\par \par \uc2 In Kiev, Ukrainian President Viktor Yushchenko dissolved parliament, blaming Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko, his estranged ally from the 2004 "Orange Revolution", for the collapse of the coalition government that emerged from that pro-Western upheaval which swept him from power.\par \par \uc2 Trading on Ukraine's PFTS stock exchange <.PFTSI><0#.PFTSI> remained suspended for a second day while Russia, one of the four biggest component economies in the MSCI emerging markets stock index, suspended share trading after steep declines in the first minutes of the session.\par \par \uc2 London-listed Russian shares however recovered to outperform global markets. The FTSE Russia index <.FTRIOB>, down 17 percent in earlier trade, closed off 2.93 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Russian assets have turned from being the darling of emerging markets investors to an obnoxious stepmother," wrote Michael Ganske, emerging markets analyst at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in London. (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy and Walter Brandimarte in New York; Aluisio Alves in Sao Paulo; Jason Lange and Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING/|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|ABX|BNX|RNP|PCM  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081011e4a8000m2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
