\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  cenbank seen reticent to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on peso\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jason Lange and Michael O'Boyle   \par \uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2008\par 22:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Oct 6 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen trying to avoid propping up the plunging peso amid the world financial crisis after years of opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have to step in if the peso plummets further and puts Mexico's hard-won economic stability at risk.\par \par \uc2 The peso nosedived 6.43 percent to 11.98 against the dollar on Monday as investors agonized over a deepening global credit crisis and prospects of less economic growth worldwide.\par \par \uc2 It was the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's worst single day since 1995, when Mexico was embroiled in an economic meltdown known as the Tequila Crisis.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's central bankers have often cited that crisis, when the economy tanked following an ultimately failed attempt by the government to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as an example of why authorities should keep their hands out of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "In Mexico they don't do that," said Bertrand Delgado, an economist at IDEAglobal.\par \par \uc2 "But if things really deteriorate far worse than we are experiencing ... they will do something and everyone will understand that."\par \par \uc2 Delgado and other analysts speculated that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided it had to protect the peso it could reinstate daily dollar auctions of international reserves that it suspended in July. Under that practice, the bank each day diverted a chunk of dollars from oil sales into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 TOUGH CALL\par \par \uc2 Dramatic changes in the value of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can wreak havoc in a country's economy.\par \par \uc2 The instability, which makes it harder to plan for the future, can dissuade investment. Rapid depreciation makes imports more expensive, stoking inflation, though a country's exports become cheaper abroad and therefore more competitive.\par \par \uc2 But a move to boost the peso's value could have big consequences because markets could put the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's will to the test by aggressively betting against the peso, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 That could put a big drain on Mexico's international reserves of $83 billion should authorities make a big effort to boost the peso.\par \par \uc2 "Those reserves ... would only last for a couple of days," said RBS Greenwich Capital economist Benito Berber.\par \par \uc2 During the Tequila Crisis, Mexico's peso-propping government eventually ran so low on reserves that it had to float the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , triggering a massive devaluation overnight.\par \par \uc2 "It's a very tough call for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist Gabriel Casillas. "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly. They have a tradition of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (Writing by Jason Lange, Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument 