\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico peso slips from 6-year high; stocks rally\b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2008\par 17:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts throughout; adds quotes and background)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 5 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso slid from a six-year high on Tuesday on speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could put the brakes on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains, while stocks jumped as lower oil prices helped boost global equity markets.\par \par \uc2 The peso <MXN=> <MEX01> lost 0.46 percent to 9.92 per dollar, erasing some of the big gains it posted on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark IPC stock index <.MXX> gained 1.01 percent to 26,755 points after falling to a six-month low on Monday.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders said the peso was knocked from its strongest level since August 2002 by profit taking and following a report from \uc2 UBS\uc2  that said \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the peso from further appreciating.\par \par \uc2 "There was a rumor that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could start buying dollars ... but I don't see the bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this moment," said the head of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading desk at a bank in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 The peso has gained in recent weeks as investors bet the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates further to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 U.S. oil prices <CLc1> fell to a three-month low below $119 a barrel at one point, helping stocks. Other commodity prices fell in tandem.\par \par \uc2 "It's a less critical situation for the American consumer, and it gives the Fed room not to raise rates," said Rodolfo Campuzano, head of analysis at brokerage firm Invex in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economy is closely tied to the United States, which receives around 80 percent of Mexican exports.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 America Movil\uc2  , the largest cell phone operator in Latin America, gained 1.76 percent to 24.83 pesos, while its New York-traded stock rose 1.34 percent to $50.07.\par \par \uc2 Cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  gained 4.36 percent to 21.08 pesos after \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  said the company may be able to successfully implement some of an announced price increase in the United States, its key market. Its stock on Wall Street advanced 4.38 percent to $21.20. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MEXICO/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080805e485001es\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
